<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Superfund Sites &amp; WA State Environmental Cleanups</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating federal and state cleanup zones across King and Snohomish counties: EPA Superfund projects, Washington Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA) database tools, and property due diligence.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Municipal Drinking Water Safety &amp; Cleanup Transparency</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Environmental cleanup tracking is actually one of the greatest tools home buyers and sellers have in Washington State. First, a vital fact: <strong>industrial river and commercial site cleanups have zero impact on your home's indoor drinking water</strong>, which is piped directly from protected, high-elevation mountain watersheds (the Cedar and Tolt rivers in King County, and Spada Lake in Snohomish County). 
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        Second, Washington operates one of the most transparent environmental tracking systems in the nation. Between federal EPA Superfund oversight and the state Department of Ecology's Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA) database, you can search any address in minutes to verify historical commercial uses, former dry cleaners or gas stations, and completed soil remedies.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Federal Superfund Sites vs. State MTCA Cleanups</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Environmental cleanup sites in Western Washington generally fall under two distinct regulatory programs depending on the size, history, and severity of the location.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">U.S. EPA Region 10</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Federal Superfund (CERCLA)</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Addresses large-scale industrial waterways and historic manufacturing hubs (Lower Duwamish Waterway, Harbor Island, Commencement Bay) requiring multi-decade sediment capping and strict federal oversight.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">WA Department of Ecology</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">State MTCA Cleanup Sites</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Addresses localized commercial properties, former corner gas stations, strip mall dry cleaners, or industrial yards remediated independently or under state supervision through soil excavation.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Federal Superfund Focus: The Lower Duwamish Waterway</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      The Lower Duwamish Waterway Superfund Site encompasses a 5.5-mile stretch of the river flowing north through South Park, Georgetown, and Tukwila into Elliott Bay. Listed by the EPA in 2001, this industrial corridor is undergoing extensive multi-agency restoration.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Habitat Restoration</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Sediment Remediation</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Involves targeted dredging of high-priority toxic spots, capping riverbed sections with engineered layers of clean sand and gravel, and monitored natural recovery.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Pollution Prevention</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Source Control</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          The WA Department of Ecology works alongside local municipalities to inspect industrial storm drains, trace upland contamination, and prevent future river re-contamination.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Public Health Directives</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Seafood Consumption Rules</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          While migratory salmon pass through safely, state health authorities strictly advise against eating resident bottom-dwelling fish (perch, sole) or crabs harvested directly from the riverbed.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">State MTCA Cleanup Designations</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Beyond large federal Superfund zones, the Washington Department of Ecology maps thousands of localized environmental sites across King and Snohomish counties under the Model Toxics Control Act.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Under Evaluation</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Active Cleanup Sites</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Properties currently undergoing environmental assessment, active soil removal, or long-term groundwater monitoring under state compliance protocols.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Formal Certification</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">No Further Action (NFA)</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Properties where soil or groundwater contamination was successfully excavated or remediated to state standards, earning formal state closure certification and clear title.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Owner-Led Mitigation</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Voluntary Cleanup (VCP)</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Independent property owner cleanups conducted proactively with formal technical review and confirmation from Department of Ecology toxicologists.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

